#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""prepare-coreml - build BMeet's speech models for the Apple Neural Engine, once, at install time.

On Linux the models run on the Intel NPU through OpenVINO. On a Mac with Apple silicon the
same models run on the Neural Engine, which only Core ML can reach, and which computes in
float16 only. ONNX Runtime's Core ML backend keeps an ONNX model in float32 and so leaves it
on the CPU (measured on an M1: TitaNet as fast with the Neural Engine allowed as without, and
bit-identical to the CPU), so this script builds real Core ML programs instead:

- ONNX models (GigaAM v3, TitaNet-small) are first given fixed input shapes and constant-folded
  by ONNX Runtime's basic optimiser (all shape arithmetic becomes constants), then translated
  op by op into a Core ML ML Program (onnx_to_mil below: the ~35 ONNX ops these two models
  use) and saved with float16 compute precision, the Neural Engine's.
- Nemotron 3 Diarization is built from its weights by transcribe/prepare-diarization's own
  graph code, given Core ML ops instead of OpenVINO's (MilOps): the same model as on the NPU.

Each model is compiled (.mlmodelc), checked against ONNX Runtime / numpy on the CPU, and
its compute plan is asked how much of it Core ML puts on the Neural Engine.

Usage:
  prepare-coreml gigaam GIGAAM_SRC OUT_DIR       sherpa-onnx's GigaAM v3 folder -> model.mlmodelc, tokens.txt
  prepare-coreml titanet TITANET.onnx OUT_DIR    -> titanet.mlmodelc (16 x 4 s per call)
  prepare-coreml nemotron MODEL.safetensors OUT_DIR   -> embedder.mlmodelc, encoder.mlmodelc, silence.npy
  prepare-coreml plan MODEL.mlmodelc             where Core ML runs each op of a model
Run with the npu-transcribe venv's Python (coremltools, onnx, onnxruntime, numpy).
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import collections
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import shutil
import sys
import tempfile
